><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20251029105051+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20251029105054+10'00'" name="828ed88d-fee5-43c5-9d74-136b62980ed9" page="6" coords="314.418793,727.639893,543.536987,727.639893,314.418793,716.850464,543.536987,716.850464,304.441254,715.677795,543.600098,715.677795,304.441254,704.888367,543.600098,704.888367,304.441254,703.715698,543.641724,703.715698,304.441254,692.926270,543.641724,692.926270,304.441254,691.753601,543.557617,691.753601,304.441254,680.964172,543.557617,680.964172,304.441254,679.791504,543.547668,679.791504,304.441254,669.002075,543.547668,669.002075,304.441254,667.829407,543.647461,667.829407,304.441254,657.039978,543.647461,657.039978,304.441254,655.867310,543.507324,655.867310,304.441254,645.077881,543.507324,645.077881,304.441254,643.961975,543.604492,643.961975,304.441254,633.172546,543.604492,633.172546,304.441254,631.999878,543.595642,631.999878,304.441254,621.210449,543.595642,621.210449,304.441254,620.037781,543.639404,620.037781,304.441254,609.248352,543.639404,609.248352,304.441254,608.075684,543.613342,608.075684,304.441254,597.286255,543.613342,597.286255,304.441254,596.113586,543.628418,596.113586,304.441254,585.324158,543.628418,585.324158,304.441254,584.151489,543.502136,584.151489,304.441254,573.362061,543.502136,573.362061,304.441254,572.189392,366.308655,572.189392,304.441254,561.399963,366.308655,561.399963" rect="304.104065,561.062805,543.984619,727.977051" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study objectivelymeasured the link between biomarkers&#xD;of stress and readiness (cortisol and testosterone) and measures&#xD;of cognition (via pupillometry) and capacity to execute motor&#xD;skill (via somatosensory processing). Results suggest that under&#xD;stressful conditions, perception may be compromised and information&#xD;processing may be delayed. Furthermore, stress&#xD;may affect our ability to execute motor skill. A novel finding&#xD;from this study was an inverse relationship seen for difference&#xD;in left pupil constriction with direct versus consensual stimulus&#xD;and cortisol variables. It was consequently theorized that&#xD;neurological dysfunction may exacerbate stress, or vice versa,&#xD;and therefore affect pupil behavior and somatosensory processing&#xD;or cognition and ability to execute motor skill, warranting&#xD;further research.</span
></p
